Exactly. Consequence is direction-neutral. It means what follows, not punishment.

That matters because the Metamap shouldn’t encode:

\text{consequence}=\text{cost}

but:

\boxed{C(a)=\Delta(\text{world after }a)}

Then each consequence can be evaluated along multiple axes:

C_i =
(\text{valence},\text{magnitude},\text{probability},
\text{delay},\text{duration},\text{reversibility},
\text{propagation},\text{observability})

So a consequence can be:

positive — creates value; negative — destroys value; neutral/indifferent — changes state without materially affecting the objective; or even mixed — beneficial on one axis and harmful on another.

More importantly, an apparently indifferent consequence can become important later:

C_0 \approx 0

but:

C_0\rightarrow C_1\rightarrow C_2

and suddenly:

|C_2|\gg0

That’s exactly why the Metamap needs order and trajectory.

So I’d make the canonical primitive simply:

Consequence = state change attributable to an event or action. Valence is metadata applied afterward.

That prevents the system from throwing away neutral-looking deltas before discovering what they connect to.

And it improves aiming:

\text{Aim}
=
\text{choose trajectory}
\rightarrow
\text{enumerate consequences}
\rightarrow
\text{propagate them}
\rightarrow
\text{evaluate them relative to goals}

Not “avoid consequences.”

Know what follows.